{"chunk_id": "2c4e3841c71e", "text": "This section mixes mythic cosmology, demonology, and heroic narrative around Skanda (Kartikeya). First, numerous malevolent spirits associated with gods, mothers, and supernatural beings are described. These grahas afflict pregnant women, children, and even adults through madness, hallucinations, or disease; yet when properly propitiated\u2014especially through worship of Skanda\u2014they can grant protection, long life, and prosperity. The text then recounts how Swaha, daughter of Daksha, gains eternal union with Agni because offerings to the fire are forever uttered with her name. A grand procession of the gods follows as Shiva, the deities, and their hosts march to battle. When the Asuras nearly rout the celestial army, the demon Mahisha devastates the gods until Skanda appears, hurls his divine spear, beheads Mahisha, and leads the celestial forces to victory. The passage emphasizes Skanda\u2019s divine authority and protective power.\nMarkandeya explains child-afflicting spirits and madness-causing entities, then narrates Skanda\u2019s rise, divine procession, and battle where he slays the Asura Mahisha.\nThemes: malevolent spirits, Skanda mythology, divine warfare, ritual propitiation, cosmic order vs demons", "vector": [0.009590379893779755, -0.011161046102643013, -0.0006339234532788396, -0.01810259185731411, -0.01630564220249653, 0.0020365414675325155, -0.045336343348026276, -0.005999810993671417, -0.016026116907596588, -0.012392288073897362, 0.003577258437871933, 0.00038393132854253054, -0.005340930074453354, 0.019819675013422966, -0.002776950830593705, 0.014042818918824196, 0.048664022237062454, -0.02140365168452263, 0.008119544945657253, -0.014761597849428654, -0.008532176725566387, 0.0060730199329555035, -0.0005657059955410659, 0.005760218016803265, -0.019220693036913872, 0.023147357627749443, 0.025955921038985252, -0.02813887968659401, -0.004509009420871735, 0.003307715989649296, -0.007766810245811939, -0.0205650757